### Description

Like `@if`, `@for` and `@switch`, a `@boundary` block copies the tag name and attributes of its single root node, so that node can be projected into a specific `ng-content` slot. When the block has more than one root node, the node goes to the default slot instead. NG8011 (`controlFlowPreventingContentProjection`) warns about this for the other blocks, but not for `@boundary`.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-card',
  template: `
    <div class="body"><ng-content /></div>
    <div class="title"><ng-content select="[card-title]" /></div>
  `,
})
export class Card {}
```

```html
<app-card>
  @boundary {
    <h2 card-title>Title</h2>
    <p>Subtitle</p>
  } @error {
    <p>Could not load the card.</p>
  }
</app-card>
```

`<h2 card-title>` ends up in `.body` instead of `.title`, and nothing is reported. With `@if (true)` in place of `@boundary`, the same template gets the NG8011 warning.

`TcbControlFlowContentProjectionOp.findPotentialControlFlowNodes` (`packages/compiler/src/typecheck/ops/content_projection.ts`) only collects `@if`, `@for` and `@switch` blocks. `@boundary` came later (#70463) and the diagnostic wasn't updated.

A related difference I noticed: `@error` blocks don't copy the tag name and attributes of their root node at all. `ingestBoundaryBlock` only does it for the main block, and the `BoundaryErrorCreate` op is emitted with a `null` tag and attributes. So a lone `<span card-title>` inside `@error` goes to the default slot, while it goes to the right slot inside an `@else`. I'm not sure whether that's intended, so I've kept it out of the fix.

#71289 has the fix and tests.

