### Is this a regression?

No

### Description


When `redirectTo` is a function, the string it returns goes through the same `:name` substitution as a static `redirectTo`. In `createQueryParams()`:

```ts
const copySourceValue = typeof v === "string" && v[0] === ":";
if (copySourceValue) {
  const sourceName = v.substring(1);
  res[k] = actualParams[sourceName];
}
```

A static `redirectTo` is written by the developer, but a function usually builds its string from the URL. Request bytes that spell `:name` then become references to another query parameter, and each one is serialized with its full value. A returned `UrlTree` is not affected.

The docs [conditional redirect example](https://angular.dev/guide/routing/redirecting-routes#conditional-redirects) interpolates `params['location']`:

```ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: "restaurant/:location/menu",
    pathMatch: "full", // added
    redirectTo: (snapshot) => {
      const location = snapshot.params["location"];
      const currentHour = new Date().getHours();

      if (snapshot.queryParams["meal"]) {
        return `/restaurant/${location}/menu/${snapshot.queryParams["meal"]}`;
      }
      if (currentHour >= 5 && currentHour < 11) {
        return `/restaurant/${location}/menu/breakfast`;
      } else if (currentHour >= 11 && currentHour < 17) {
        return `/restaurant/${location}/menu/lunch`;
      } else {
        return `/restaurant/${location}/menu/dinner`;
      }
    },
  },
  { path: "restaurant/:location/menu/breakfast", component: Menu },
  { path: "restaurant/:location/menu/lunch", component: Menu },
  { path: "restaurant/:location/menu/dinner", component: Menu },
  { path: "", redirectTo: "/restaurant/downtown/menu", pathMatch: "full" },
  { path: "**", component: PageNotFound },
];
```

Compared with the guide this adds `pathMatch: "full"` and the `**` route, and either one alone is enough: with neither, the redirect route prefix-matches its own destinations and the query is dropped on the second hop.

A request like:

```text
/restaurant/a%2Fmenu%2Flunch%3F0=:z&1=:z& ... &hq=:z&/menu?z==========...===&meal=x
```

puts `?0=:z&1=:z&...` inside `location`, so every key becomes a reference to `z`, and `%2Fmenu%2Flunch` keeps the redirect on a destination route. The output is references times the length of `z`, both paid from the same request, so it grows with the square of the request size.

I tested Angular **22.2.2 on Node.js 24.16.0**. One request, default heap, median of 3 runs:

|  Request | References |   `Location` | Equal-size control |
| -------: | ---------: | -----------: | -----------------: |
|  8,000 B |        675 |  8,056,107 B |            4,032 B |
| 16,000 B |      1,341 | 32,112,927 B |            8,046 B |

Concurrent requests through Nginx that terminate the SSR worker:

| V8 old space | 8,000 B | 16,000 B |
| -----------: | ------: | -------: |
|      256 MiB |      12 |        3 |
|      512 MiB |      22 |        5 |

All failures were V8 heap OOMs:

```text
JavaScript heap out of memory
OOMKilled=false
```

The byte-identical control, with `@z` instead of `:z`, completes successfully and Angular remains healthy. Both sizes fit Node's default 16 KiB header limit.

Skipping the `:` substitution for function results brings the candidate down to the size of its control, and a normal `?meal=lunch` redirect is unchanged.

